  <widgets>
    <view x="480" wrappingAllowedX="true" y="10" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="0" height="484" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="4.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="true" width="324" tickCounterLabel="year" maxPycor="119" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="79" minPxcor="0"></view>
    <slider x="15" step="1" y="10" max="36" width="181" display="fertility-ends-age" height="50" min="26" direction="Horizontal" default="34.0" variable="fertility-ends-age"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.001" y="65" max="0.2" width="181" display="fertility" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.155" variable="fertility"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="1" y="120" max="40" width="181" display="death-age" height="50" min="26" direction="Horizontal" default="38.0" variable="death-age"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.01" y="175" max="1" width="181" display="harvest-adjustment" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.54" variable="harvest-adjustment"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.01" y="230" max="1" width="181" display="harvest-variance" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.4" variable="harvest-variance"></slider>
    <plot x="20" autoPlotX="true" yMax="10.0" autoPlotY="true" y="465" xMin="0.0" height="260" legend="true" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" width="455" display="Population">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="sim. households" color="-2674135" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot total-households</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="historical data" color="-13345367" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot historical-total-households</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="carrying-capacity" color="-7500403" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot potential</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <slider x="15" step="0.0001" y="295" max="0.01" width="185" display="recovery-rate" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.0" variable="recovery-rate" units="per year"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.0001" y="355" max="0.02" width="185" display="depletion-rate" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.0" variable="depletion-rate" units="per year"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.01" y="410" max="1" width="185" display="soil-heterogeneity" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.4" variable="soil-heterogeneity"></slider>
    <button x="210" y="30" height="35" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="90">setup</button>
    <button x="210" y="70" height="35" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="90">go</button>
    <switch x="305" y="75" height="40" on="true" variable="historic-view?" width="147" display="historic-view?"></switch>
    <switch x="305" y="30" height="40" on="false" variable="setup-yield-fix?" width="115" display="setup-yield-fix?"></switch>
    <chooser x="310" y="120" height="60" variable="map-view" current="0" width="147" display="map-view">
      <choice type="string" value="zones"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="water sources"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="yield"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="occupancy"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="soil quality"></choice>
    </chooser>
    <monitor x="205" precision="17" y="115" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100">potential</monitor>
    <monitor x="210" precision="17" y="180" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="sites available">farm-sites-available</monitor>
    <plot x="215" autoPlotX="true" yMax="300.0" autoPlotY="false" y="265" xMin="0.0" height="175" legend="true" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" width="230" display="available sites vs carrying capacity">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Available sites" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot farm-sites-available</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Carrying capacity" color="-2674135" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>plot potential</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <monitor x="320" precision="17" y="190" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100">fit-correlation</monitor>
    <plot x="500" autoPlotX="true" yMax="1.5" autoPlotY="true" y="500" xMin="0.0" height="175" legend="false" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" width="230" display="resource pressure (households / capacity)">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="default" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update><![CDATA[ifelse potential > 20
  [ plot total-households / potential ]
  [ plot 0 ]]]></update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <slider x="490" step=".1" y="680" max="2" width="250" display="degradation-factor" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="degradation-factor"></slider>
    <slider x="740" step="1" y="505" max="100" width="50" display="run-seed" height="225" min="1" direction="Vertical" default="1.0" variable="run-seed"></slider>
    <slider x="820" step="1" y="55" max="4" width="250" display="crowding-limit" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="crowding-limit"></slider>
    <slider x="825" step="0.05" y="120" max="1" width="250" display="intensification-return" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.5" variable="intensification-return"></slider>
    <switch x="820" y="185" height="40" on="true" variable="uplands-fix?" width="147" display="uplands-fix?"></switch>
    <switch x="820" y="250" height="40" on="true" variable="soil-on?" width="147" display="soil-on?"></switch>
    <switch x="820" y="295" height="40" on="true" variable="crowding-on?" width="147" display="crowding-on?"></switch>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## ARTIFICIAL ANASAZI: BASELINE + DYNAMIC SOIL + CROWDING

## THE BASELINE

All versions of this model are built from one baseline: the Artificial Anasazi model from the NetLogo Models Library (Stonedahl & Wilensky 2010), which adapts Janssen's (2009) NetLogo replication of the original Ascape model (Dean et al. 2000; Axtell et al. 2002), converted to NetLogo 7.0.4, plus a small set of fixes that make the model work as intended. The fixes change results very little. Their purpose is to give later modifications a starting point that does what the model's documentation says it does.

* **Uplands zone name** (`uplands-fix?`, default ON). The map loader names the arable-uplands zone "Uplands", but `calculate-yield` tested for "Upland", so the arable-uplands patches never received a PDSI or hydro value. OFF reproduces the published behavior.
* **Setup base yield** (`setup-yield-fix?`, default OFF). The published setup computes base yield without `harvest-adjustment`, so initial placement sees yields that are too high until the first tick recomputes them. ON applies the adjustment at setup. OFF keeps the published behavior.
* **Initial household count.** `total-households` was never assigned in setup, so the first recorded value was 0.
* **Carrying capacity reporting.** The published `potential` is read before the year's harvest is recomputed, so it lags one tick. The baseline re-measures it each tick and keeps the published value as `potential-lagged`.
* **Seed control and instruments** (observation only). `run-seed` seeds the random number generator. Added reporters give per-year series of simulated households, archaeological households and carrying capacity, the year of extinction, correlation and RMS error against the archaeological series, and peak-window reporters for the two-peak comparison of Janssen (2009, section 4.13).

Code comments mark these changes [BASELINE FIX] and [BASELINE].

## WHAT THIS VERSION ADDS: DYNAMIC SOIL AND CROWDING, EACH SWITCHABLE

This version contains both modifications below, each behind its own switch. `soil-on?` enables dynamic soil quality. `crowding-on?` enables scarcity-driven plot sharing. With both OFF the model reproduces the baseline. With only `soil-on?` ON it gives the soil-only model, and with only `crowding-on?` ON it gives the crowding-only model. The soil and crowding sliders have no effect while their switch is OFF. With both ON, depletion scales with the number of households working a plot. Code comments mark these changes [SOIL] and [CROWDING].

### Dynamic soil

Each cultivated plot loses `depletion-rate` of its relative quality per year, and every plot regrows toward its setup value following Janssen (2010, Ecology and Society 15(2):19, eq. 4). `degradation-factor` is his lambda: 0 gives logistic regrowth, and higher values suppress regrowth at low quality, which mimics erosion. `recovery-rate` sets the maximum annual regrowth as a fraction of the setup quality. `soil-heterogeneity` is the standard deviation of the setup quality draw (the baseline uses `harvest-variance`). A "soil quality" view is added to `map-view`.

### Scarcity-driven crowding

When no empty plot is available anywhere, a relocating or newly formed household may join an occupied plot, up to `crowding-limit` households, if its share of the plot's output still meets the nutrition need. A plot worked by n households produces base-yield * n^alpha, so each household's share is base-yield * n^(alpha - 1), where `intensification-return` is alpha. Carrying capacity is also reported in crowding-adjusted form (`potential-crowded`).

## WHAT IS IT?

This model simulates the population dynamics in the Long house Valley in Arizona between 800 and 1400. It is based on archaeological records of occupation in Long House Valley and shows that environmental variability alone can not explain the population collapse around 1350. The model is a replication of the work of Dean et al. (see references below).

## HOW IT WORKS

### Agents and the environment

Each agent represents a household of five persons. Each household makes annual decisions on where to farm and where to settle. A household has an age, and a stock of food surplus
previous years. Each cell represents a 100 meter by 100 meter space. Each cell is within one of the different zones of land: General Valley Floor, North Valley Floor, Midvalley Floor, Arable Uplands, Uplands Non-Arable, Kinbiko Canyon or Dunes. These zones have agricultural productivity that is determined by the Palmer Drought Severity Index (PDSI).

### Initialization

The initial number of households is 14. Each household is initialized by setting a household age from the uniform distribution [0, 29] and by setting the value of the corn stocks by the uniform distribution [2000, 2400].  The quality of the soil of the cells is initialized by adding a number drawn from the normal distribution with standard deviation specified by HARVEST-VARIANCE.


### Process overview

Every year the following sequence of calculations is performed:

  1. calculate the harvest for each household
  2. if agent derives not sufficient food from harvest and storage or the age is beyond maximum age of household the agent is removed from the system
  3. calculate the estimated harvest for next year based on corn in stock and actual harvest from current year
  4. agents who expect not to derive the required amount of food next year will move to a new farm location.
  5. find a farm plot
  6. find a plot to settle nearby
  7. if a household is older than the minimum fission age, there is a probability pf, that a new household is generated. The new household will derive an endowment of a fraction of the corn stock.
  8. update water sources based on input data
  9. the household's age increases by one year

### Design concepts
  * _Adaptation_. Agents adjust the location of farming and housing if they expect that current location is not sufficient next year.
  * _Prediction_. The prediction of harvest next year is the same as the experienced harvest from this year.
  * _Fitness_. Fitness of agent is determined by the amount of harvest derived and amount of corn in storage. An agent is either considered to be "fit" or not fit. If not sufficient food is available, it is removed from the system.
  * _Interaction_. Agents do not interact directly. Indirectly they interact by occupying potential farm plot. Agents represent households. In the model, reproduction is modeled at the household level.  Based on the age of the household and the availability of food, a household may give birth to an offspring household, which is representing a daughter who leaves the house and starts a new household (immediately with 3 children that require 160 kg corn per year).  These abstraction is simplistic, but still captures the essence of reproduction and population growth.
  * _Stochasticity_.  Initial conditions of cells and agents, and the order in which agents are updated.

## HOW TO USE IT?

If you click on SETUP the model will load the data files and initialize the model. If you click on GO the simulation will start. You can adjust a number of sliders before you click on SETUP to initialize the model for different values.
The slider HARVEST-ADJUSTMENT is the fraction of the harvest that is kept after harvesting the corn. The rest is assumed the be lost in the harvesting process. The default value is around 60%. If you increase this number much more agents than the historical record is able to live in the valley.
The slider HARVEST-VARIANCE is used to create variation of quality of cells and temporal variability in harvest for each cell. If you have variance of the harvest some agents are lucky one year and need to use their storage another year. If there is no variance many agents will leave the valley at once when there is a bad year.
The slider DEATH-AGE represents the maximum number of years an agent can exists. A lower number will reduce the population size.
The slider FERTILITY-ENDS-AGE represents the maximum age of an agent to be able to produce offspring. A lower number will reduce the population size.
The slider FERTILITY is the annual probability an agent gets offspring. A lower probability will reduce the population size.


POPULATION GRAPH: the blue line shows the historical data, while the red line the simulated population size.

**Maps**. With the MAP-VIEW chooser you can select different ways to view the landscape on the right.

  * ZONES: this will show you the different land cover zones.
    * Black: General Valley Floor
    * Red: North Valley Floor
    * White: Mid and North Dunes
    * Gray: Midvalley Floor
    * Yellow: Non-Arable Uplands
    * Blue: Arable Uplands
    * Pink: Kinbiko Canyon
  * WATER SOURCES: this will show you the different surface water sources like springs. This is changing over time due to input data on retrodicted environmental history. Households will chose new locations based on being nearby water sources.
  * YIELD: Each cell is colors on the amount of yield can be derived from the cell. The more yield the lighter the color.
  * OCCUPANCY: yellow cells are use for agriculture, red cells for settlements

If HISTORIC-VIEW? is on you will see the locations of settlements according to the data. The settlements are shown as houses, and the size (area of the house shape) is proportional the number of households on that location.

